# 1-to-8 Demultiplexer using Verilog

## Description

A **1-to-8 Demultiplexer (DEMUX)** is a digital circuit that takes **one data input** and routes it to **one of eight outputs** using **three select lines**.

## Inputs

* `din` – 1-bit data input
* `sel[2:0]` – 3-bit select input

## Output

* `y[7:0]` – 8-bit output

## Block Diagram

```text
                  ┌─────────────────┐
       DIN ──────►│                 │────► Y0
       S2  ──────►│                 │────► Y1
       S1  ──────►│    1-to-8       │────► Y2
       S0  ──────►│    DEMUX        │────► Y3
                  │                 │────► Y4
                  │                 │────► Y5
                  │                 │────► Y6
                  │                 │────► Y7
                  └─────────────────┘
```

## Truth Table

| DIN | SEL | Output Y |
| --- | --- | -------- |
| 1   | 000 | 00000001 |
| 1   | 001 | 00000010 |
| 1   | 010 | 00000100 |
| 1   | 011 | 00001000 |
| 1   | 100 | 00010000 |
| 1   | 101 | 00100000 |
| 1   | 110 | 01000000 |
| 1   | 111 | 10000000 |

## Expected Console Output

```text
Time=0  | DIN=1 | SEL=000 | Y=00000001
Time=10 | DIN=1 | SEL=001 | Y=00000010
Time=20 | DIN=1 | SEL=010 | Y=00000100
Time=30 | DIN=1 | SEL=011 | Y=00001000
Time=40 | DIN=1 | SEL=100 | Y=00010000
Time=50 | DIN=1 | SEL=101 | Y=00100000
Time=60 | DIN=1 | SEL=110 | Y=01000000
Time=70 | DIN=1 | SEL=111 | Y=10000000
Time=80 | DIN=0 | SEL=010 | Y=00000000
```

## Project Structure

```text
1-to-8-Demultiplexer/
│
├── demux_1to8.v
├── tb_demux_1to8.v
└── README.md
```

## Simulation

Using **Icarus Verilog**:

```bash
iverilog -o demux_sim demux_1to8.v tb_demux_1to8.v
vvp demux_sim
```

For waveform generation, add `$dumpfile` and `$dumpvars` to the testbench and open the generated `.vcd` file using **GTKWave**.

## Result

The 1-to-8 demultiplexer successfully routes the input `din` to the selected output `y[0]` through `y[7]`.

## Applications

* Data routing
* Communication systems
* Digital switching
* Memory selection
* Digital logic design

